---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const tanks = assertion('the-county-had-two-tank-installations')

const books = assertion('the-war-turned-the-locomotive-works-books')

const booksSpec = bar(books.figures.map((f) => f.label), books.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Locomotives ordered',
  labelValues: true,
})
---

<Article slug="two-tank-plants">
  <AssertionCard assertion={tanks} />
  <p>
    The county's best-known industrial fact is that it builds tanks, and the corpus could not say
    since when. It can now say something better than a date, which is that the question has two
    answers and they are not the same kind of thing. The Lima Locomotive Works — a private company
    with its works here and its offices in New York — put up a $290,000 factory building in
    February 1941 and was scheduled to begin turning out medium tanks that autumn, alongside
    Chrysler, American Locomotive, Baldwin, Pullman Standard and Pressed Steel Car.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Lima Tank Depot was something else. Still under construction in January 1943, run under
    contract by United Motors Service, Inc., it existed to receive vehicles built at arsenals all
    over the country "in a raw state of completion", fit the modifications a particular theatre
    needed, test them on its own proving ground and put them on road trains for the ports. Its
    officer in charge called it the central supply base for combat vehicles: troops in the field
    did not order from a factory, they ordered from Washington, and Washington ordered from Lima.
    Its workforce came in from thirteen counties.
  </p>
  <p>
      Two government-owned, contractor-operated tank installations in one small Ohio city are very
      plausibly one place, and for a long time no source said so.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      Whether either stands where the tank plant stands today was declined here for two phases.
    </Gloss>
  <p>
    There is one now, and it says they are not. The Recorder's abstract for the ground the plant
    occupies shows it in private hands through the whole of the Second World War — mortgaged in
    1940, 1941 and 1944, leased to a fishing and game club in April 1940, crossed by pipeline
    easements its owners granted in 1939, 1940 and 1949 — and conveyed to the United States in
    five deeds between 20 and 28 June 1951, each recorded the day it was signed or the day
    after. The Depot was under construction in January 1943. A farm was standing on this ground
    when it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the county's tank plant is not a wartime installation at all, on this evidence. It is a Korean War one, bought six years after the war everyone tells the story about.
  </p>
    <Gloss>
      The refusal above held for two phases and was right to.
    </Gloss>

  <h2>What the war did to a locomotive builder's books</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={books} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="locomotive-orders"
    title="Locomotives ordered from the Lima Locomotive Works, 1939 and 1940"
    caption="Two years, reported to the county in February 1941. Not a series: the corpus holds no other year of this company's orders, and two points are a comparison rather than a trend."
    columns={['Year', 'Locomotives ordered']}
    rows={books.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="locomotive-orders" set:html={JSON.stringify(booksSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The county's largest employer went from a net loss of $134,326 to a net profit of $87,007 in
    one year, on orders that more than tripled, and booked eighteen more locomotives in the first
    seven weeks of 1941. Then it took a tank contract. Everything this corpus knows about the
    commercial side of Allen County's war comes from one page of a village weekly, which is worth
    keeping in view when reading it.
  </p>
</Article>
